+// Writing to $zero is a no-op on this hardware -- the register reads as zero
+// however it is assigned -- and `lw $zero, 0(a0)` is a real idiom, used to
+// touch a cache line or hold a load slot. The generator renders $zero as the
+// literal 0, so the natural output is `0 = MEM_W(...)`, which no C compiler
+// will take. Discard the result but keep the expression, which is what the
+// instruction does.
+static bool writes_to_zero(const std::string& output) {
+    return output == "0";
+}
